2026-08-02 17:14:04,329 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/embeddings', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-41a1295b-6d2e-4696-8313-b5adb73f2af4', 'post_parser': <function Embeddings.create.<locals>.parser at 0x7f1414efe840>, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'input': 'Critical Flaw Allowed to Azure Cosmos DB Pwnage\n\n“The Config Store was itself a Cosmos DB database, meaning it could be queried with the full flexibility of CosmosDB’s SQL engine. It also meant that the Cosmos Master Key could retrieve its primary key – enabling attackers that exploited CosmosEscape to list all accounts in a region, or query by specific tenant ID to identify a specific organization’s databases,” the company explains. An attacker with the master key and access to the configuration store could enumerate all Cosmos DB accounts, filter them, and retrieve a target’s primary key to gain full read and write access to all their databases. According to Wiz, the attack could also be mounted against private and network-isolated Cosmos DB accounts, as well as Microsoft’s own databases. Wiz reported the vulnerability to Microsoft in November 2025.', 'model': 'text-embedding-3-small', 'encoding_format': 'base64'}}
