2026-08-02 17:14:33,467 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/embeddings', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f1f8a119-3625-4d69-98ea-8248822acf92', 'post_parser': <function Embeddings.create.<locals>.parser at 0x7f14254a2c00>, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'input': 'Russian Hackers Exploit Microsoft OWA Flaw to Keep Mailbox Access After Credential Rotation\n\nThe messages have been found to mimic informational emails on topics such as supply chain analyses, research updates, and metrics for tourism or gas markets. The use of such generic emails is once again a consistent hallmark in the threat actor\'s half-click exploit chains, as the idea here is to lend them an illusion of legitimacy and not arouse a victim\'s suspicion by purposefully excluding any URLs or attachments. In doing so, the likelihood that a recipient opens and reads the message increases, effectively firing the exploit for CVE-2026-42897 in the process. "This allows a loader piece of JavaScript to use the onload= event handler to parse the rest of the message body, assemble a Base64 fragment, and execute it as encoded JavaScript," Proofpoint explained. "The initial exploit trigger and relevant payload blobs are stored in the social media icons shown in the message body HTML. The next-stage payload data is stored after # symbols, which the browser stops at when parsing images from Base64."', 'model': 'text-embedding-3-small', 'encoding_format': 'base64'}}
